#{[17592186557314 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186557317 :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ H & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186557325 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\displaystyle \\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186557325 :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186557447 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\nxy=3,\\,xy=7,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=4,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=5\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=\\frac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=xy$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=3\\,⟹\\,u=3 \\\\  & xy=7\\,⟹\\,u=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{y}{x}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{y}{x}=4\\,⟹\\,v=4 \\\\  & \\frac{y}{x}=5\\,⟹\\,v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 7,\\,4\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/3d/3dc524083463c8c370ff1f79d68093f0d3f2c2fe1ceb5cd47824782e35a0bac9.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{x^2} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}+\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{2v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3}^{7}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{4}^{5}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (7-3)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186557450 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\nxy=2,\\,xy=5,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=2,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=3\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=\\frac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=xy$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=2\\,⟹\\,u=2 \\\\  & xy=5\\,⟹\\,u=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{y}{x}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{y}{x}=2\\,⟹\\,v=2 \\\\  & \\frac{y}{x}=3\\,⟹\\,v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 5,\\,2\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/2c/2cdd0431c256b72ded23c14fac4098b518a46603d46d5dfd238c54b3a46c66db.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{x^2} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}+\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{2v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (5-2)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186557452 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\nxy=2,\\,xy=5,\\,\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=2,\\,\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=3\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=xy$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=2\\,⟹\\,u=2 \\\\  & xy=5\\,⟹\\,u=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=2\\,⟹\\,v=2 \\\\  & \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=3\\,⟹\\,v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 5,\\,2\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/2c/2cdd0431c256b72ded23c14fac4098b518a46603d46d5dfd238c54b3a46c66db.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} & -\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{3v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (5-2)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186557458 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\nxy=1,\\,xy=4,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=3,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=4\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=\\frac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=xy$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=1\\,⟹\\,u=1 \\\\  & xy=4\\,⟹\\,u=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{y}{x}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{y}{x}=3\\,⟹\\,v=3 \\\\  & \\frac{y}{x}=4\\,⟹\\,v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,3\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/88/88750e49134feaa087b1f48ddee12faeb30ca8a31dd3b5bebb989f4c7c891ae7.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{x^2} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}+\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{2v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (4-1)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186557460 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}y=4,\\,{x}^{2}y=7,\\,xy=4,\\,xy=8\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}y,\\,v=xy\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u={x}^{2}y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{2}y=4\\,⟹\\,u=4 \\\\  & {x}^{2}y=7\\,⟹\\,u=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=xy$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=4\\,⟹\\,v=4 \\\\  & xy=8\\,⟹\\,v=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,4\\le u\\le 7,\\,4\\le v\\le 8\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/47/475635e9575bd2f30069f2ee5f3b29bd6d7bf47db0cb6d5a96f981e36b8117c6.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2xy & {x}^{2} \\\\ y & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{7}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{4}^{8}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)\\cdot (8-4) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186557470 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}y=3,\\,{x}^{2}y=6,\\,xy=2,\\,xy=6\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}y,\\,v=xy\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u={x}^{2}y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{2}y=3\\,⟹\\,u=3 \\\\  & {x}^{2}y=6\\,⟹\\,u=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=xy$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=2\\,⟹\\,v=2 \\\\  & xy=6\\,⟹\\,v=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 6,\\,2\\le v\\le 6\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/a4/a42d50fe797c9450b27acde75ac7b69ed3e4ea5acc6c9dc93cd6df18b9ed1882.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2xy & {x}^{2} \\\\ y & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{2}^{6}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)\\cdot (6-2) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186557473 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\nxy=4,\\,xy=5,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=3,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=5\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=\\frac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=xy$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=4\\,⟹\\,u=4 \\\\  & xy=5\\,⟹\\,u=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{y}{x}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{y}{x}=3\\,⟹\\,v=3 \\\\  & \\frac{y}{x}=5\\,⟹\\,v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,4\\le u\\le 5,\\,3\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/56/560d4d074d9d07eb034d92b8f1dfabfc3e59d659c91d80c3fedf68367a575e41.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian 